        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the <strong>power of product rule for exponents</strong>. </p>",
            "readable_text": "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the power of product rule for exponents.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To demonstrate, let&#x27;s simplify the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (5 \\times 3)^4]</span> by applying the power of product rule:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: (5 \\times 3)^4] by applying the power of product rule:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (5 \u00d7 3)^{4}|=; (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; (5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5)_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 5^{4} \u00d7 3^{4}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (5 \u00d7 3)^{4}|=; (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; (5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5)_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 5^{4} \u00d7 3^{4}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the power of product rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: (5 \\times 3)^4] by applying the power of product rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (5 \u00d7 3)^{4}|=; (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; (5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5)_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 5^{4} \u00d7 3^{4}]"
      },
