    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the power of product rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: (5 \\times 3)^4] by applying the power of product rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (5 \u00d7 3)^{4}|=; (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; (5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5)_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3)_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 5^{4} \u00d7 3^{4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power of Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: (3\\times 2)^2] as the product of two powers.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise the product of two numbers to the power of [MATH: 2,] we can raise each number to the power of [MATH: 2,] and then multiply:\n\n[MATH: (3\\times 2)^2=3^2\\times 2^2]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (6 \u00d7 5)^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6^{5} \u00d7 5]\nb. [MATH: 6 \u00d7 5^{5}]\nc. [MATH: 6^{5} \u00d7 5^{5}]\nd. [MATH: 6 \u00d7 5 + 5]\ne. [MATH: 6 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (2 \u00d7 3)^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2^{4} \u00d7 3^{4}]\nb. [MATH: 2^{4} \u00d7 3]\nc. [MATH: 2 \u00d7 3 + 4]\nd. [MATH: 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 4]\ne. [MATH: 2 \u00d7 3^{4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power of Product Rule to Expressions with Fractions\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(2\\times \\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2] as the product of two powers.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise the product of two numbers to the power of [MATH: 2,] we can raise each number to the power of [MATH: 2,] and then multiply:\n\n[MATH: \\left(2\\times \\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2=2^2 \\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((3)/(7) \u00d7 (1)/(2))^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (3)/(7) \u00d7 ((1)/(2))^{4}]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(7^{4} \u00d7 2^{4})]\nc. [MATH: (3^{4})/(7 \u00d7 2)]\nd. [MATH: ((3)/(7))^{4} \u00d7 ((1)/(2))^{4}]\ne. [MATH: ((3)/(7))^{4} \u00d7 (1)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (9 \u00d7 (2)/(5))^{6} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9 \u00d7 (2^{6})/(5)]\nb. [MATH: (9^{6} \u00d7 2^{6})/(5)]\nc. [MATH: 9^{6} \u00d7 (2)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: 9^{6} \u00d7 ((2)/(5))^{6}]\ne. [MATH: 9 \u00d7 ((2)/(5))^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power of Product Rule to Expressions with Negative Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(-2\\times 4\\right)^3] as the product of two powers.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise the product of two numbers to the power of [MATH: 3,] we can raise each number to the power of [MATH: 3,] and then multiply:\n\n[MATH: \\left(-2\\times 4\\right)^3=\\left(\\left(-2\\right)\\times 4\\right)^3= \\left(-2\\right)^3\\times 4^3]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(5) \u00d7 7)^{6} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(5))^{6} \u00d7 7^{6}]\nb. [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(5))^{6} \u00d7 7]\nc. [MATH: 5^{6} \u00d7 7]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(5) \u00d7 7^{6}]\ne. [MATH: 5 \u00d7 7^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 7 \u00d7 3)^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(7) \u00d7 3^{5}]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 7 \u00d7 3^{5}]\nc. [MATH: (\u2212 7)^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}]\nd. [MATH: (\u2212 7)^{5} \u00d7 3]\ne. [MATH: ((1)/(7))^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}]"
